  <!-- PART 1: STATUTORY -->
  
<div class="rule-section" aria-hidden="true"><div class="rule-arr-l"></div><div class="rule-line"></div><div class="rule-arr-r"></div></div>
<section id="statutory">
    <div class="section-label">Part 1 — Employee Benefits</div>
    <h2>What Indian law actually requires you to provide</h2>
    <p class="section-intro">These are not optional. They're legal obligations under Indian labour law, each with its own registration deadline, filing cadence, and penalty for non-compliance. Most MNCs treat this layer as the hard part. It isn't. Registration is procedural. What catches people out is the sequencing.</p>

    <div class="callout callout-coral">
      <span class="callout-icon">⚑</span>
      <div><strong>Labour Code 2025:</strong> Four new codes consolidated 29 prior laws, effective 21 November 2025. Basic salary must now be at least 50% of CTC. Salary structures built around suppressing EPF liability are no longer compliant. Gratuity eligibility for fixed-term employees drops from 5 years to 1. Restructure payroll before your first hire, not after.</div>
    </div>

    <div class="table-scroll"><table class="statutory-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Scheme</th>
          <th>Who it applies to</th>
          <th>Employer contribution</th>
          <th>Employee contribution</th>
          <th>What it covers</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>EPF</strong><br><span style="font-size:11px;color:var(--ink-muted)">Employees' Provident Fund</span></td>
          <td>Establishments with 20+ employees. Employees earning basic+DA up to ₹15,000/month</td>
          <td>12% of basic+DA (8.33% → EPS, balance → EPF)</td>
          <td>12% of basic+DA</td>
          <td>Retirement savings, withdrawable for housing/medical</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>ESI</strong><br><span style="font-size:11px;color:var(--ink-muted)">Employees' State Insurance</span></td>
          <td>Establishments with 10+ employees. Employees earning ≤₹21,000/month gross</td>
          <td>3.25% of gross wages</td>
          <td>0.75% of gross wages</td>
          <td>Medical care, sickness cash benefit, maternity, occupational injury. <em>Note: ESI replaces employer obligation for group health for in-scope employees.</em></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Gratuity</strong><br><span style="font-size:11px;color:var(--ink-muted)">Payment of Gratuity Act, 1972</span></td>
          <td>Establishments with 10+ employees. Employee eligible after 5 continuous years (1 year for fixed-term post-2025 reforms)</td>
          <td>15 days' last drawn salary × years of service. Capped at ₹20 lakh</td>
          <td>Nil</td>
          <td>Lump-sum on resignation, retirement, death, or disability</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Workmen's Compensation / EC</strong><br><span style="font-size:11px;color:var(--ink-muted)">Employees' Compensation Act, 1923</span></td>
          <td>All employees not covered by ESI, typically those earning above the ESI threshold or in non-ESI-notified areas</td>
          <td>Employer bears full cost of insurance premium</td>
          <td>Nil</td>
          <td>Compensation for occupational injury, partial/permanent disability, death in course of employment</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>EDLI</strong><br><span style="font-size:11px;color:var(--ink-muted)">Employees' Deposit Linked Insurance</span></td>
          <td>All EPF-covered employees (administered via EPFO)</td>
          <td>0.5% of basic wages (capped at ₹75/month per employee)</td>
          <td>Nil</td>
          <td>Life insurance cover of up to ₹7 lakh payable to nominee on death in service</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Maternity benefit</strong><br><span style="font-size:11px;color:var(--ink-muted)">Maternity Benefit Act, 1961</span></td>
          <td>Establishments with 10+ employees. Women employees with 80+ days of work in past 12 months</td>
          <td>Full pay for 26 weeks (first two children); 12 weeks for third child onwards</td>
          <td>Nil (ESI-covered employees receive benefit via ESIC instead)</td>
          <td>Paid maternity leave; nursing breaks; crèche obligation for 50+ employee establishments</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table></div>
  </section>


  <!-- PART 2: VOLUNTARY EB -->
  
<div class="rule-section" aria-hidden="true"><div class="rule-arr-l"></div><div class="rule-line"></div><div class="rule-arr-r"></div></div>
<section id="voluntary-eb">
    <div class="section-label">Part 2 — Voluntary Employee Benefits</div>
    <h2>The stack your candidates already expect</h2>
    <p class="section-intro">None of these are legally required. No regulator will fine you for skipping them. But every credible candidate you recruit at ₹8 LPA and above will check for them. Candidates from IT, BFSI, and consulting arrive with a clear mental model of what a serious employer carries. This is the actual floor, and it's higher than the compliance floor.</p>

    <div class="policy-card">
      <div class="policy-card-head">
        <div class="policy-icon teal">🏥</div>
        <div class="policy-title-block">
          <div class="policy-name">Group Health Insurance (GHI)</div>
          <div class="policy-tagline">Floater or individual sum insured; employee + dependants</div>
        </div>
        <div class="policy-badges">
          <span class="badge badge-recommended">Recommended</span>
          <span class="badge badge-gc">GCC standard</span>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="policy-divider"></div>
      <div class="policy-body">
        <div class="policy-field">
          <label>Who needs it</label>
          <p>Any employer whose workforce sits above the ₹21,000/month ESI threshold, which is most MNC white-collar hires. ESI covers medical needs for lower-wage employees; above that ceiling, your statutory health obligation is zero. GHI fills that gap.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="policy-field">
          <label>Minimum group size</label>
          <p>7 employees to qualify for a standard group policy. Below this, insurers require individual retail policies (30–50% higher premium, with waiting periods).</p>
        </div>
        <div class="policy-field">
          <label>What it covers</label>
          <p>Inpatient hospitalisation, pre-existing conditions from day one (no waiting period in group plans), maternity, daycare procedures, cashless claims at network hospitals. Family floater typically covers spouse and two children; parents as opt-in top-up.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="policy-field">
          <label>What good looks like</label>
          <p>India median: ₹5,00,000. Global startups in India: ₹10,00,000. The 90th percentile sits at ₹8,00,000 regardless of headcount. Small companies can match large ones if they choose to.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="policy-field">
          <label>IRDAI 2024 changes</label>
          <p>Pre-existing disease waiting period capped at 36 months (was 48). Moratorium reduced from 8 to 5 years. No upper age limit on new group policy enrolments. Retail PED waits now extend to 36 months, making group cover materially more valuable than a retail policy.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="policy-field">
          <label>Tax</label>
          <p>Premium is deductible for the employer under Section 37(1). 18% GST applies. Not treated as a perquisite under Section 17(2), so it doesn't increase the employee's taxable income.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="policy-field full">
          <label>Insurer options</label>
          <p>
            <span class="trigger-tag">ICICI Lombard</span>
            <span class="trigger-tag">HDFC ERGO</span>
            <span class="trigger-tag">Bajaj Allianz</span>
            <span class="trigger-tag">Star Health</span>
            <span class="trigger-tag">Niva Bupa</span>
            <span class="trigger-tag">Aditya Birla Health Insurance</span>
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="policy-card">
      <div class="policy-card-head">
        <div class="policy-icon coral">🛡️</div>
        <div class="policy-title-block">
          <div class="policy-name">Group Personal Accident (GPA)</div>
          <div class="policy-tagline">Accidental death, disability, income replacement</div>
        </div>
        <div class="policy-badges"><span class="badge badge-recommended">Recommended</span></div>
      </div>
      <div class="policy-divider"></div>
      <div class="policy-body">
        <div class="policy-field">
          <label>What it covers</label>
          <p>Accidental death at 5–10× annual salary, permanent total and partial disability, weekly income replacement for temporary disablement. Often includes a medical expense reimbursement component.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="policy-field">
          <label>Relation to WC/EC</label>
          <p>GPA provides broader cover than mandatory Workmen's Compensation: it covers non-occupational accidents and is not restricted to the workplace. MNCs typically hold both.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="policy-field full">
          <label>Why it matters</label>
          <p>EDLI under EPF maxes out at ₹7 lakh, less than one year's salary for most MNC hires. GPA is what makes the accidental death benefit credible. At the 90th percentile, companies offer ₹50,00,000 or 3–5× CTC.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="policy-card">
      <div class="policy-card-head">
        <div class="policy-icon blue">💼</div>
        <div class="policy-title-block">
          <div class="policy-name">Group Term Life (GTL)</div>
          <div class="policy-tagline">Death benefit; employer-paid life cover</div>
        </div>
        <div class="policy-badges">
          <span class="badge badge-recommended">Recommended</span>
          <span class="badge badge-gc">GCC standard</span>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="policy-divider"></div>
      <div class="policy-body">
        <div class="policy-field">
          <label>Typical structure</label>
          <p>Death-in-service benefit of 3–5× annual CTC, fully employer-paid. No medical underwriting for groups of sufficient size. Often extended to include a critical illness or accidental death rider.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="policy-field">
          <label>Where adoption stands</label>
          <p>36% of sub-100 companies carry it; 76% at 750+. Notably, global startups in India are at 30%, well behind their GHI provision. GTL is where most MNCs have a gap.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="policy-card">
      <div class="policy-card-head">
        <div class="policy-icon amber">🩺</div>
        <div class="policy-title-block">
          <div class="policy-name">OPD / Wellness Benefits</div>
          <div class="policy-tagline">Outpatient, mental health, preventive care</div>
        </div>
        <div class="policy-badges"><span class="badge badge-recommended">Recommended</span></div>
      </div>
      <div class="policy-divider"></div>
      <div class="policy-body">
        <div class="policy-field">
          <label>What it covers</label>
          <p>Outpatient consultations, diagnostics, pharmacy reimbursements, mental health support, telemedicine access, annual preventive check-ups. These are the benefits employees use every year, not once in a decade.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="policy-field">
          <label>Where adoption stands</label>
          <p>9% of sub-100 companies offer OPD; 60% of 750+ companies do. Global startups in India are already at 60%, double the India median of 16%. The gap is closing fast.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="policy-field full">
          <label>The hospitalisation blind spot</label>
          <p>Standard GHI covers inpatient events. But Plum's telehealth data shows what employees actually need day to day: mental health (19% of all consultations), dermatology (18%), OB-GYN (10%). None of these are covered by a base GHI plan.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>


